Technical constraints that aren’t obvious just by reading the source

- Bridge‑transport rules – api/bridge_link.py now decides whether the engine can talk to the MT5 bridge over HTTP or only receive push data.  The helper is_inbound_http_allowed() blocks any private‑LAN URL (e.g. http://192.168.x.x) on a cloud host, forcing the bridge to open an outbound WebSocket.  This rule is enforced in several places (health check, should_poll_http, resolve_bridge_link).  
- Ingest‑fresh tracking – A thread‑safe dictionary (_last_ingest) records the timestamp of the last snapshot received from the bridge.  Functions touch_ingest(), is_ingest_fresh() and the new “heartbeat” path (touch_heartbeat()) are used by LiveSyncService to decide when to poll the bridge and when to mark the bridge as offline.  The logic is hidden behind the bridge_link module and not visible in the API routes.  
- Account‑switch detection & buffer clearing – LiveSyncService._detect_account_switch() compares the current bridge account (login, server, company) with the previously seen one.  If a switch is detected it clears all chart and equity buffers (_clear_buffers).  This protects the engine from mixing data from two different MT5 accounts.  
- Session‑document migration – api/trading_session.py now adds three new top‑level fields (trade_mode, automation, day_lock) and normalises older documents with _normalize_session().  The migration is performed on‑the‑fly when a session is read, so older records in MongoDB continue to work without a separate migration script.  
- Static‑key authentication – api/request_auth.py validates every request against two secrets (KASSANDRA_AUTH_SECRET and KASSANDRA_AUTH_SALT).  The “master‑key / normal‑key” model is enforced everywhere (e.g. _require_master(), _require_tenant()).  This is a security constraint that lives outside the business logic.  
- Isolation lock for cloud deployments – The commit series on 2026‑06‑28 introduced a lock file (runtime_store.isolation_lock_path) and the LiveSyncService._run_trading_cycle() checks it before starting a cycle.  This prevents two engine processes from running simultaneously on the same EC2 instance.  

---

Business constraints that drive the technical choices

---

Compatibility requirements that limit what can be changed

1. Bridge JSON contract – The external “victoria‑bridge” expects a specific schema (account, positions, quotes, charts, bridge_connected).  All changes to LiveSyncService.apply_bridge_push() must preserve those keys; otherwise the bridge will reject the payload.  

2. Authentication API – Clients (Kassandra Client, external dashboards) send the X‑Kassandra‑Request‑Id header and a token that is validated against the master/normal secret.  Renaming the header, changing the token format, or removing the master‑key path would break every existing consumer.  

3. Session document shape – Older sessions stored in MongoDB lack the new trade_mode, automation, and day_lock fields.  The on‑read normalisation (_normalize_session()) guarantees backward compatibility, so any schema change must continue to provide defaults or a migration path.  

4. Public‑vs‑private bridge URLs – The rule that only localhost URLs are allowed for inbound HTTP (is_inbound_http_allowed()) is relied upon by the health‑check endpoint and by the client‑side error messages (_resolve_bridge_link).  Removing or relaxing this check would require coordinated changes in the bridge deployment scripts and in any on‑premise installations.  

5. Static‑key auth model – All endpoints (/api/account/mode, /api/trading/trade-mode, etc.) check request_auth.validate_key().  Switching to OAuth, JWT, or a database‑backed user model would require a sweeping rewrite of every route and of the client libraries that currently embed the simple token.  

6. File‑based configuration paths – The launcher (run.sh, run.ps1, run.bat) and the README assume the config files live under config/ and that .env is present at the repository root.  Moving these files would break the “one‑command” start‑up flow and would require updates to many scripts and to the Docker/EC2 deployment instructions.  

7. Isolation‑lock file location – The lock path is hard‑coded (/opt/kassandra/trading_engine/.lock).  Production scripts (systemd unit, deployment docs) reference that exact path.  Changing it would need updates to the service definition and to any monitoring tooling.  

Bottom line:  
The engine’s design is deliberately constrained to keep the product usable by non‑engineers, to protect live capital on shared cloud hosts, and to remain compatible with an existing MT5 bridge and a simple token‑based auth scheme.  Any refactor that touches authentication, bridge JSON, session schema, or the inbound‑HTTP rule will have to be coordinated with the bridge team, the deployment scripts, and the client applications that rely on the current contract.
